{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"0517d843578fdae1","name":"card_event_armaments_eg","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Britain/specialCards/card_event_armaments_eg","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"ARMAMENTS","text":"Deal 4 damage to any target. Deal 8 if this card spent 4+ turns in your deck.","flavorText":"The bomb loads of four-engine British bombers during WWII varied depending on the type of aircraft, the target, and the mission. However, here are some examples of the bomb loads of some of the most common four-engine bombers used by the Royal Air Force:\r\n\r\nAvro Lancaster: The Lancaster had a maximum bomb load of up to 22,000 pounds (9,980 kg), although the typical bomb load for most missions was around 14,000 pounds (6,350 kg) of bombs.\r\n\r\nHandley Page Halifax: The Halifax had a maximum bomb load of up to 14,500 lb (6,600 kg), although the typical bomb load for most missions was ca.5,800 pounds (2,600 kg).\r\n\r\nShort Stirling: The Stirling had a maximum bomb load of up to 14,000 pounds (6,350 kg) of bombs, although the typical bomb load for most missions was around 8,000 pounds (3,600 kg).\r\n\r\nBoeing B-17 Flying Fortress (used by the RAF's No. 100 Group): The B-17 had a maximum bomb load of  8,000 pounds (3,600 kg) , although the typical bomb load for most missions was around 4,000-pound (1,800-kg).\r\n\r\nIt's worth noting that the weight and type of bombs used by these bombers varied depending on the mission and the target. For example, bombers on night bombing missions tended to carry more incendiary bombs, while those on daylight precision bombing missions often carried heavier high-explosive bombs.","faction":"Britain","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":4,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"newCardText":{"Namespace":"","Key":"6AB6A7D546440BDBC6393D8715858558","SourceString":"Reduce all numbers by one for each turn spent in deck. Deal {0} damage.","LocalizedString":"R